 {"id": "PEDS-U436", "ch": 54, "n": 1, "title": "Definition, Types & Stages", "sec": "Shock — definition, types and stages · p256", "guide": "Shock is circulatory dysfunction with decreased oxygen delivery and cellular perfusion insufficient for metabolic demands. Hypovolemic shock (m/c — hemorrhage, dehydration) joins cardiogenic (congenital heart disease, myocarditis, cardiomyopathy), distributive (anaphylactic, neurogenic) and septic shock (features of all three). Compensated shock maintains vital brain–heart–kidney function via sympathetic activation (↑ SVR, ↑ contractility, blood diverted from skin); prolonged shock decompensates into multiorgan dysfunction. Circulatory adequacy is judged by capillary refill time — blanching the thumb — normal ≤ 2 sec, > 3 sec in shock.", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-001", "PEDS-C54-002", "PEDS-C54-003", "PEDS-C54-004", "PEDS-C54-005", "PEDS-C54-006", "PEDS-C54-007", "PEDS-C54-008", "PEDS-C54-009", "PEDS-C54-010", "PEDS-C54-011", "PEDS-C54-012", "PEDS-C54-013", "PEDS-C54-014", "PEDS-C54-015", "PEDS-C54-016", "PEDS-C54-017", "PEDS-C54-018", "PEDS-C54-019", "PEDS-C54-020", "PEDS-C54-021", "PEDS-C54-022", "PEDS-C54-023", "PEDS-C54-024", "PEDS-C54-025", "PEDS-C54-026"]},
 {"id": "PEDS-U437", "ch": 54, "n": 2, "title": "Compensated vs Decompensated Shock", "sec": "Compensated vs decompensated shock · p256", "guide": "Compensated shock shows agitation or confusion, increased heart rate, CRT > 3 sec, normal urine output, normal BP and increased respiratory rate. Decompensated shock shows drowsiness, markedly increased heart rate, CRT > 5 sec, oliguria/anuria, decreased BP and increased respiratory rate.", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-027", "PEDS-C54-028", "PEDS-C54-029", "PEDS-C54-030", "PEDS-C54-031", "PEDS-C54-032", "PEDS-C54-033", "PEDS-C54-034", "PEDS-C54-035", "PEDS-C54-036", "PEDS-C54-037", "PEDS-C54-038", "PEDS-C54-039", "PEDS-C54-040"]},
 {"id": "PEDS-U438", "ch": 54, "n": 3, "title": "Hypovolemic & Cardiogenic Shock", "sec": "Hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock · p257", "guide": "Hypovolemic shock is suspected with fluid loss or ↓ oral intake: dry mouth, ↓/absent tears, sunken eyes, oliguria with ↑ creatinine and ↑ urea, and ↓ CVP as lost volume lowers preload. Cardiogenic shock is impaired contractility with venous pooling: ↑ CVP, ↑ JVP, S3 gallop, congestive hepatomegaly, plus primary cardiac clues (murmurs, pericarditis friction rub) and a ventricular-failure diagram.", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-041", "PEDS-C54-042", "PEDS-C54-043", "PEDS-C54-044", "PEDS-C54-045", "PEDS-C54-046", "PEDS-C54-047", "PEDS-C54-048", "PEDS-C54-049", "PEDS-C54-050", "PEDS-C54-051", "PEDS-C54-052", "PEDS-C54-053", "PEDS-C54-054", "PEDS-C54-055", "PEDS-C54-056", "PEDS-C54-057", "PEDS-C54-058", "PEDS-C54-059", "PEDS-C54-060", "PEDS-C54-061", "PEDS-C54-062", "PEDS-C54-063", "PEDS-C54-064"]},
 {"id": "PEDS-U439", "ch": 54, "n": 4, "title": "Distributive & Septic Shock", "sec": "Distributive and septic shock · p257", "guide": "Distributive shock is warm shock — warm extremities, bounding pulses, flash CRT (< 1 sec) — from vasodilatation due to ↓ SVR with ↓ afterload and ↓ cardiac output. Septic shock splits into cold type (cold extremities, CRT > 3 sec, feeble/decreased pulses) and warm type (warm extremities, flash CRT, bounding pulses).", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-065", "PEDS-C54-066", "PEDS-C54-067", "PEDS-C54-068", "PEDS-C54-069", "PEDS-C54-070", "PEDS-C54-071", "PEDS-C54-072", "PEDS-C54-073", "PEDS-C54-074", "PEDS-C54-075", "PEDS-C54-076", "PEDS-C54-077", "PEDS-C54-078", "PEDS-C54-079", "PEDS-C54-080"]},
 {"id": "PEDS-U440", "ch": 54, "n": 5, "title": "Shock Algorithm — ABC, Fluids & Pressors", "sec": "Shock algorithm — ABC, fluids and pressors · p258", "guide": "Recognise shock quickly; stabilise A, B, C with high-flow O2 and IV access (intraosseous if IV is inaccessible for 60 seconds); check and treat hypoglycemia and sepsis. Give an initial 10–20 ml/kg bolus over 15–20 min, then 20 ml/kg over 15–20 min repeated up to 60 ml/kg in the first hour; no response for 60 min is fluid-refractory shock. Cold shock needs vasodilation (epinephrine or dopamine 5–9 µg/kg/min); warm shock needs vasoconstriction (norepinephrine or dopamine ≥ 10 µg/kg/min); epi/norepi start at 0.05 and titrate to 3 µg/kg/min. Catecholamine-resistant shock gets corticosteroids (if adrenal insufficiency is suspected), cardiac monitoring and transfusion to Hb > 10 g/dl.", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-081", "PEDS-C54-082", "PEDS-C54-083", "PEDS-C54-084", "PEDS-C54-085", "PEDS-C54-086", "PEDS-C54-087", "PEDS-C54-088", "PEDS-C54-089", "PEDS-C54-090", "PEDS-C54-091", "PEDS-C54-092", "PEDS-C54-093", "PEDS-C54-094", "PEDS-C54-095", "PEDS-C54-096", "PEDS-C54-097", "PEDS-C54-098", "PEDS-C54-099", "PEDS-C54-100", "PEDS-C54-101", "PEDS-C54-102", "PEDS-C54-103", "PEDS-C54-104", "PEDS-C54-105", "PEDS-C54-106"]},
 {"id": "PEDS-U441", "ch": 54, "n": 6, "title": "Resistant Shock Branches & ECMO", "sec": "Resistant shock branches and ECMO · p258", "guide": "Cold shock with normal BP gets vasodilators (nitroprusside/milrinone, nitroglycerin/levosimendan); cold shock with ↓ BP gets titrated epinephrine to the highest dose plus norepinephrine to normalise diastolic BP, then milrinone/levosimendan if there is no response; warm shock with ↓ BP gets volume titration to normal, added vasopressin for vasoconstriction, and milrinone/levosimendan if cardiac output falls. Persistent failure prompts a search for reversible causes (pericardial effusion, pneumothorax) and then ECMO — oxygenation of blood through a machine outside the body.", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-107", "PEDS-C54-108", "PEDS-C54-109", "PEDS-C54-110", "PEDS-C54-111", "PEDS-C54-112", "PEDS-C54-113", "PEDS-C54-114", "PEDS-C54-115", "PEDS-C54-116", "PEDS-C54-117", "PEDS-C54-118", "PEDS-C54-119", "PEDS-C54-120", "PEDS-C54-121", "PEDS-C54-122", "PEDS-C54-123", "PEDS-C54-124", "PEDS-C54-125", "PEDS-C54-126"]},
 {"id": "PEDS-U442", "ch": 54, "n": 7, "title": "IO Sites, Rules of 60, Fluids, Vasoactives & Endpoints", "sec": "IO sites, fluids, vasoactives and endpoints · p259", "guide": "IO sites are proximal tibia and distal femur. Rules of 60: IO if IV is unavailable in 60 sec, boluses up to 60 ml/kg in the first 60 min, vasoactives 60 min after starting fluids. Crystalloids (↓ price, easy availability) beat colloids (long-term paediatric risk unknown); normal saline 0.9% and Ringer lactate — isotonic solutions only. Sepsis is managed with a 3rd-generation cephalosporin + vancomycin. Vasopressors alter resistance (epinephrine, norepinephrine, vasopressin); inotropes raise contractility (epinephrine, dobutamine, milrinone/PDE-III inhibitor, levosimendan/Ca sensitizer); vasodilators dilate vessels (nitroglycerine, nitroprusside). Endpoints: normal HR/BP, urine > 1 ml/kg/hr, CRT < 2 sec, normal mentation.", "qs": ["PEDS-C54-127", "PEDS-C54-128", "PEDS-C54-129", "PEDS-C54-130", "PEDS-C54-131", "PEDS-C54-132", "PEDS-C54-133", "PEDS-C54-134", "PEDS-C54-135", "PEDS-C54-136", "PEDS-C54-137", "PEDS-C54-138", "PEDS-C54-139", "PEDS-C54-140", "PEDS-C54-141", "PEDS-C54-142", "PEDS-C54-143", "PEDS-C54-144", "PEDS-C54-145", "PEDS-C54-146", "PEDS-C54-147", "PEDS-C54-148", "PEDS-C54-149", "PEDS-C54-150", "PEDS-C54-151", "PEDS-C54-152", "PEDS-C54-153", "PEDS-C54-154"]},
